      "title": "Hemispheric Diffusion: A Compositional Generative Policy for Coordinated Bimanual Manipulation",
      "takeaway": "HemiDiff addresses bimanual imitation policies that fuse all sensory inputs early, which the authors argue can amplify modality conflicts and fail under asymmetric or degraded observations. The method decomposes multimodal conditioning into independent modality experts and recomposes their denoising predictions via an asymmetric perception router that assigns arm-specific modality weights at inference, with a coordination energy expert guiding denoising toward demonstration-consistent relative motion. Experiments on RLBench2 and a real bimanual robot report a 76% average success rate on real-world tasks, 19 percentage points above the strongest baseline, with stated robustness to occlusion, sensor degradation, and viewpoint perturbations. While reading, inspect how the router weights are computed at inference and how the coordination energy term is defined and weighted against the diffusion objective.",
